    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p14-q17-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the smallest four-digit number that has exactly three factors.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1369$.\n\nA number with exactly three factors is the square of a prime.",
          "worked": "A number with exactly three factors has the form $p^2$, where $p$ is prime; its factors are $1,p,p^2$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}31^2&=961<1000\\\\37^2&=1369\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe consecutive primes $31$ and $37$ straddle the required threshold, so the smallest four-digit number is $1369$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-continuation-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Suppose $a\\in\\mathbb{N}$ has three factors and $b\\in\\mathbb{N}$ has five factors. Their product $ab$ has seven factors. Find the smallest possible value of $ab$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$64$.\n\nThe primes must coincide, giving $ab=p^6$; choose $p=2$.",
          "worked": "The divisor counts give $a=p^2$ and $b=q^4$ for primes $p,q$. If $p\\ne q$, then $ab=p^2q^4$ has $(2+1)(4+1)=15$ factors. Therefore $p=q$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}ab&=p^2p^4\\\\ab&=p^6\\end{align*}$$\n\nThis has seven factors. The smallest prime is $2$, so the minimum is $ab=2^6=64$, attained by $a=4$ and $b=16$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-continuation-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Suppose $a\\in\\mathbb{N}$ has three factors and $b\\in\\mathbb{N}$ has four factors. Their product $ab$ has eight factors. Find the smallest possible value of $ab$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$24$.\n\nThe product must have the form $p^3r$ for distinct primes; choose $p=2$ and $r=3$.",
          "worked": "Since $a$ has three factors, $a=p^2$ for a prime $p$. A number with four factors is either $q^3$ or $qr$, where $q,r$ are distinct primes.\n\nIf $b=q^3$, then $p=q$ gives $ab=p^5$ with six factors, while $p\\ne q$ gives $ab=p^2q^3$ with $3\\times4=12$ factors. Neither gives eight factors.\n\nThus $b=qr$. If $p$ differs from both $q$ and $r$, the product has $3\\times2\\times2=12$ factors. Hence $p$ equals one of them, giving $ab=p^3r$ with $p\\ne r$ and $4\\times2=8$ factors.\n\nUse the two smallest primes, placing the larger exponent on the smaller prime:\n\n$$ab=2^3\\times3=24.$$\n\nThis is attained by $a=4$ and $b=6$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      }
    ]
  },
